Deliver orchestration mail to a headless-served terminal

Push-on-idle delivery is leaf-shaped: every step resolves the pane through the renderer
graph. `orca serve` publishes one explicitly empty graph — the web client's syncWindowGraph
is a stub, so a browser never publishes leaves — and a pane served that way exists only as a
PTY record. Nothing resolved, so nothing was ever pointed: in the tile a coordinator's run
mail sat unread until someone went and told that agent to check its own inbox.

The PTY record carries the same pane identity and the same live titles the leaf does, so it
is projected into `OrchestrationMailboxLeaf` and the one delivery implementation — with its
in-flight serialization, waiter filtering, sequence guard and submit deadline — serves both.
The three resolvers consult it only after the graph misses, and the new idle trigger is
gated on `getLeavesForPty` being empty, so a desktop pane is never pointed twice.

To test, have a coordinator send run mail to an idle agent in the tile and watch the pointer
arrive without anyone prompting that agent. Covered by
`headless-orchestration-delivery.test.ts`.
